2) Caching to scale
“Cache active schema per formId” — what exactly?

Idea:
Store the result of “active version” lookup in cache:

Key: activeVersion:<formId>
Value: the whole active FormVersion DTO (id, version, schemaJson, publishedAt…)

Then GET /active becomes:

cache hit → return immediately

cache miss → query DB, store into cache, return

Why it helps

Active version doesn’t change frequently. It changes only when you publish.
So caching is very effective.

Cache invalidation rule

When you publish new version:

invalidate activeVersion:<formId>
or overwrite it with new active version

3) “Cache schema by versionId” — what exactly?

Idea:
Each version is immutable (best practice). So it’s perfect for caching.

Key: formVersion:<versionId>
Value: schema JSON + metadata

Then GET /versions/{versionId} becomes:

cache hit → return schema instantly

cache miss → query DB, cache it, return

Why it helps

Diff calculations or viewing old versions would repeatedly call version fetch

Since versions never change, you can cache them longer (hours/days)

Invalidation rule

If versions are immutable: no invalidation needed (except TTL expiry)

Example caching strategy (recommended)
Caches

active-version cache (key: formId)

version-by-id cache (key: versionId)

TTL suggestions

Active version: TTL 5–30 minutes (plus explicit eviction on publish)

Version by ID: TTL 6–24 hours (or even days)

4) Why two caches then?

Because the lookup keys are different:

/active is “by formId” → cache activeVersion:<formId>

/versions/{id} is “by versionId” → cache versionById:<versionId>

Even if the payload shape is similar, the lookup and invalidation rules are different:

active changes on publish → must evict activeVersion:<formId>

a version snapshot doesn’t change → safe to cache for long time by versionId

5) Quick rule-of-thumb

/active = “current published version for new users”

/versions/{id} = “exact historical snapshot used by existing users / diff / admin”

So both endpoints are required for your enterprise “sync new version / keep old version” behavior.

